# 6. Taints & Tolerations

### Taint

Node par lagta hai aur unwanted Pods ko repel karta hai.

```text
Node
 ↓
Taint
 ↓
No normal Pod
```

### Toleration

Pod ko tainted node par schedule hone ki permission deta hai.

```text
Tainted Node
      +
Pod Toleration
      ↓
Pod can run
```

Example use case:

```text
GPU Node
 ↓
GPU workload only
```

---

# 7. Cordon vs Drain

### Cordon

Node ko **unschedulable** mark karta hai.

```bash
kubectl cordon <node>
```

New Pods schedule nahi honge.

Existing Pods running reh sakte hain.

### Drain

Node ke workloads ko gracefully evict karta hai.

```bash
kubectl drain <node>
```

Maintenance ke liye commonly use hota hai.

```text
Cordon → New Pods stop
Drain  → Existing Pods remove/evict
```

---

# 8. Pod Disruption Budget (PDB)

PDB voluntary disruptions ke time minimum availability maintain karne mein help karta hai.

Example:

```yaml
minAvailable: 2
```

Matlab at least 2 Pods available rehne chahiye during allowed voluntary disruption.

Or:

```yaml
maxUnavailable: 1
```

Maximum 1 Pod unavailable ho sakta hai.

Useful during:

* Node drain
* Cluster maintenance
* Node upgrades

---

# 9. Deployment Rolling Update

Two important settings:

### maxSurge

Update ke time kitne **extra Pods** temporarily create ho sakte hain.

```text
replicas = 4
maxSurge = 25%
```

→ Extra Pods allowed during rollout.

### maxUnavailable

Update ke time maximum kitne Pods unavailable ho sakte hain.

```text
maxUnavailable = 1
```

→ Maximum 1 Pod unavailable.

